      },
      "worked": "Arrange the $n$ men in a circle in $(n-1)!$ ways. They create $n$ gaps. To keep women apart, each gap can contain at most one woman.\n\nIf $k>0$, there are more women than gaps, so $P(\\text{no adjacent women})=0$.\n\nIf $k\\le0$ and both group sizes are positive, choose $n+k$ of the $n$ gaps and arrange the women in them. All $(2n+k-1)!$ circular arrangements are equally likely.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(\\text{no adjacent women})&=\\frac{(n-1)!\\binom{n}{n+k}(n+k)!}{(2n+k-1)!}.\\end{align*}$$\nIn particular, when $k=0$,\n$$\\begin{align*}P(\\text{no adjacent women})&=\\frac{(n-1)!n!}{(2n-1)!}.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
